@meta
id: 17-11
title: The edges of what you just put
chapter: 17
type: lesson
skills: mark-backtick, marks-jump
par: 26
minutes: 5
vscode: paste, then drag-select the pasted lines and press Tab
open: payment.service.ts
vimrc: set shiftwidth=4 expandtab

@brief
Somebody drafted a status check at the very bottom of this file,
outside the class, flush against the left margin. It belongs in
`refund`, directly after the `const response` line, with a blank line
on either side. `charge` and `capture` show what it should look like.

Move it. Delete the draft as a paragraph, put it in the right place,
then indent the lines you just put, and only those lines, by one
level. Do not count them and do not select them. Vim has already
marked where the put text starts and where it ends.

@keys
| `'[` | first line of the last change or put |
| `']` | last line of the last change or put |
| `` `[ `` `` `] `` | the same, exact character |
| `>` | indent, given a motion |
| `dap` | delete a paragraph |

@hints
After `p`, the cursor is on the first line that was put. You need a
motion from there to the last line that was put.
--
`']` is a motion to the last line of the text you just put, so `>']`
indents from the cursor to the end of it.
--
`G` `dap` takes the draft and its leading blank line. `?refund(` Enter,
`j` `p` drops it under the `const response` line. Then `>']`.

@solution
    G dap ?refund(<Enter> jp >'] :wq<Enter>

Every time you change or put text, vim records where it starts in
`[` and where it ends in `]`. They are the third pair of automatic
marks, after `.` and `^`, and they turn "the thing I just pasted" into
a motion. `>']` indents it. `=']` would re-indent it. `d']` would
delete it again. `:'[,']s/foo/bar/` substitutes inside it and nowhere
else, which is how you rename things in a block you just copied
without touching the original.

The draft's leading blank line came along with `dap`, which is what
made the spacing right: the register held blank, `if`, `throw`, `}`,
and putting it after the `const response` line gave a blank above the
block and the existing blank below it. When a paragraph is the last
one in the file, `ap` reaches back for the blank line above it instead
of forward.

The lesson set `shiftwidth` to four so that one `>` moves the draft
from the margin to method-body depth. In your own projects it is
whatever the codebase uses, and `>` moves by that.

@start payment.service.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';

@injectable()
export class PaymentService {
  public async charge(args: { orderId: string; amount: number }) {
    const response = await this.http.post('/charges', args);

    if (response.status !== 200) {
      throw exception('CHARGE_FAILED');
    }

    return response.data;
  }

  public async refund(orderId: string) {
    const response = await this.http.post('/refunds', { orderId });

    return response.data;
  }

  public async capture(orderId: string) {
    const response = await this.http.post('/captures', { orderId });

    if (response.status !== 200) {
      throw exception('CAPTURE_FAILED');
    }

    return response.data;
  }
}

if (response.status !== 200) {
  throw exception('REFUND_FAILED');
}

@expected payment.service.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';

@injectable()
export class PaymentService {
  public async charge(args: { orderId: string; amount: number }) {
    const response = await this.http.post('/charges', args);

    if (response.status !== 200) {
      throw exception('CHARGE_FAILED');
    }

    return response.data;
  }

  public async refund(orderId: string) {
    const response = await this.http.post('/refunds', { orderId });

    if (response.status !== 200) {
      throw exception('REFUND_FAILED');
    }

    return response.data;
  }

  public async capture(orderId: string) {
    const response = await this.http.post('/captures', { orderId });

    if (response.status !== 200) {
      throw exception('CAPTURE_FAILED');
    }

    return response.data;
  }
}
